  "scars": [
    {
      "description": "Engineered 'Method A' Vertical Hanging Fixture to utilize gravity against glass-transition sag during rubberized paint cure cycle [4, 8].",
      "label": "The Banana Defect",
      "value": "2.50mm to &lt;0.5mm"
    },
    {
      "description": "Engaged domestic vendor Mass Precision for manual offset welding to bridge Pilot when overseas automation failed due to standoff density [9, 10].",
      "label": "The No-Bid Shock",
      "value": "Line Down Averted"
    },
    {
      "description": "Emergency redesign of front bolster to create recessed access for headphone jack, converting RTF liability to Field Replaceable Unit [3, 11].",
      "label": "The Trap Door Protocol",
      "value": "4.8% Failure Rate"
    }
  ],
  "metrics": {
    "quotes": [
      "The Units that are due were in the paint shop and it was discovered that the C'snks were omitted. This is a manual operation. [13]",
      "twist due to paint cure effect? curing fixture? [14]",
      "Modification lead time is longer. To avoid any unfavorable status during pilot run in Vtech, the modification will be delayed... [15]"
    ]
  },
  "forensic_summary": {
    "trigger": "The RoHS (Lead-Free) transition spawned a 'Crisis of Materiality.' The new compliance-grade plastics exhibited a catastrophic differential shrinkage rate compared to the legacy resins, causing the main console bucket (a massive single-shot part) to warp by 2.5mm. This rendered the unit unsellable and threatened to kill the product line 8 years into its lifecycle.",
    "intervention": "With no budget for new hard tooling ($250k+), I engineered a 'Process-as-Architecture' solution. I implemented the 'Vertical Gravity Cure', a novel post-molding fixture strategy where parts were hung vertically while cooling through their glass-transition phase, allowing gravity to naturally nullify the internal stresses.",
    "result": "This zero-cost process intervention reduced the warpage from 2.5mm to <0.5mm, bringing the parts back into spec. The C|24 successfully launched as a RoHS-compliant unit without a single dollar spent on new steel tooling, extending its revenue lifespan by another 4 years."
  },
